{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016af2CjtEFSY2na2vvdDaLZ","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Excellent! Now I have all the information needed. Let me compile the findings:\n\n## Summary of Findings\n\n### 1. No cmd/pwd Directory Exists\nThe cmd/pwd directory does not exist in this repository. The available cmd/ utilities are:\n- arch, basename, cat, dirname, du, false, head, hostid, hostname, logname, ls, mktemp, nproc, seq, sponge, tee, true, uname, version, wc, yes\n\n### 2. InstallSIGPIPEHandler Signature\nLocated at `/private/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/5230/pkg/sys/sigpipe.go`:\n\n```go\nfunc InstallSIGPIPEHandler() {\n\tc := make(chan os.Signal, 1)\n\tsignal.Notify(c, syscall.SIGPIPE)\n\tgo func() {\n\t\t<-c\n\t\tos.Exit(0)\n\t}()\n}\n```\n\nThis function installs a SIGPIPE handler that exits with status 0 when stdout/stderr is closed, matching GNU coreutils behavior. It's safe to call multiple times and can be called early in main().\n\n### 3. Cmd/ Implementation Patterns\n\nI'll show three complete examples of small utilities:\n\n#### **Example 1: cmd/logname/main.go** (Medium Complexity)\nPattern: Custom flag parsing, environment variable reading, error handling\n- Imports: fmt, os, strings, sys\n- Flag handling: Manual switch statement parsing\n- Main flow: parseFlags() → validate args → read LOGNAME env var → output\n- Error handling: Check for write errors on Stdout\n\n#### **Example 2: cmd/true/main.go** (Minimal)\nPattern: Simplest possible command\n- Imports: fmt, os, sys\n- Flag handling: Simple switch on first arg only\n- Main flow: Check for --help/--version → exit 0\n- Error handling: Check fmt.Fprint errors before os.Exit\n\n#### **Example 3: cmd/false/main.go** (Minimal)\nPattern: Mirror of true, exits with failure code\n- Imports: fmt, os, sys\n- Flag handling: Simple switch on first arg only\n- Main flow: Check for --help/--version (still exit 1) → exit 1\n- Error handling: Minimal (just fmt.Fprint without error check)\n\n#### **Example 4: cmd/hostname/main.go** (Higher Complexity)\nPattern: Label-based flag parsing with goto, OS call, output handling\n- Imports: fmt, os, strings, sys\n- Flag handling: Loop-based parsing with goto done pattern\n- Main flow: Parse flags (with --) → validate no extra args → os.Hostname() → output\n- Error handling: Check os.Hostname() error, check fmt.Fprintln error\n\n#### **Example 5: cmd/yes/main.go** (Most Complex)\nPattern: Functional decomposition, block buffering, signal handling\n- Imports: errors, fmt, os, strings, syscall, sys\n- Flag handling: Separate handleSpecialFlag() function\n- Main flow: handleSpecialFlag() → buildOutputLine() → fillBlock() → writeLoop()\n- Error handling: EPIPE detection with errors.Is()\n- Key pattern: Builds output block for efficient buffering (8192 bytes)\n\n### Directory Structure Pattern\nEach cmd has:\n- `main.go` - the implementation\n- `*_test.go` - unit tests (logname_test.go, true_test.go, hostname_test.go, etc.)\n\n### Common Patterns Across All Implementations\n1. **First line in main()**: Always call `sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler()`\n2. **Help/Version**: Defined as `helpText` and `versionText` constants\n3. **Imports**: Always include the local `github.com/petar-djukic/go-unix-utils/pkg/sys` package\n4. **Error output**: Use `fmt.Fprintf(os.Stderr, ...)` for errors\n5. **Error handling**: Check errors on Stdout writes and exit with appropriate code\n6. **Flag parsing**: Either manual switch statements or loop-based parsing with labeled breaks\n7. **Exit codes**: 0 for success, 1 for errors/help requests (except true/false which have special semantics)"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"78aabc7d-55d4-4690-a9a1-d1597569ac6c","uuid":"56912825-beac-4ae6-85e6-09ae6026736c","timestamp":"2026-05-14T23:24:53.339Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"I need to find patterns used in existing cmd/ implementati