{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_014wsH4MBWuW8VAgdEKNm5TB","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have a good understanding of the patterns. Let me implement `cmd/pwd/main.go`. The key requirements:\n\n1. R1.1: Default is -P (physical mode), prints CWD + newline, exit 0\n2. R1.2: -L/--logical prints PWD env var if valid (names current dir, no `.`/`..` components), falls back to physical\n3. R1.3: -P/--physical resolves all symlinks\n4. R1.4: When both -L and -P given, last one wins\n\n\n5. R2.1: Positional operands → stderr error, exit 1\n6. R2.2: Unknown flags → stderr error, exit 1\n\nFor flag parsing, I need to handle `-L`, `--logical`, `-P`, `--physical`, `--help`, `--version`, `--` for end of flags, combined short flags like `-LP` where the last one wins, and reject unknown flags with an error. For logical mode, I need to validate that PWD is set, resolves to the same inode as the current directory, and contains no `.` or `..` components—otherwise fall back to physical mode.\n\nFor physical mode, I'm checking how GNU pwd validates the PWD environment variable: it must start with `/`, contain no `.` or `..` components, and resolve to the same device and inode as the actual working directory. In Go, `os.Getwd()` returns the physical path by calling the system's `getcwd()`, which already handles symlink resolution, so I should be able to use that directly for comparison.\n\nNow I'm writing the implementation... Writing the argument parser... Continuing with the short flag parsing logic...\n\nI'm validating the logical PWD by checking if it's an absolute path, ensuring it doesn't contain dot components like `.` or `..`, and then verifying that the PWD environment variable actually points to the same directory as the current working directory by comparing their inode and device numbers.\n\nNow I'm reviewing the function sizes to make sure they're all reasonable — checking that `parseArgs`